<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyawswa I of Pinya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1344">1344</a>–<a href="/years/1350">1350</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyawswa I was king of Pinya in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1344 to 1350, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a>, founder of the kingdom. His reign fell in a period of rivalry between Pinya and the breakaway kingdom of Sagaing, ruled by his half-brother's line. He was succeeded by his sons, and Pinya was sacked by Shan forces in 1364, after which Ava replaced it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyawswa-i-of-pinya.jpg" alt="Kyawswa I of Pinya" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Richard Carnac Temple · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ngazishin_Nat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
